    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Expression of the bacterial protein ActA is essential for Listeria monocytogenes actin-based polymerization, motility, and subsequent cell-to-cell spread. Despite in depth characterization of the role of ActA in L. monocytogenes motility, little is currently known regarding the effect of ActA expression on bacterial growth rate. Characterization of bacterial growth rates of L. monocytogenes strains expressing or lacking ActA sheds light on the impact of the proteins expression on bacterial growth. Induced ActA expression in extracellular growth curves suggests a minimal difference in replication rates. However, cell lysis of infected endothelial cells suggests that intracellular production of ActA is correlated with a decrease in the intracellular growth rate of L. monocytogenes. This impact was found to be independent of bacterial motility associated with ActA expression. Because ActA expression is a pivotal part in L. monocytogenes pathogenisis, understanding the effect of ActA on bacterial replication during intracellular invasion sheds light on the infectious life cycle of the bacterium.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
